{
  "policy_id": "ld-94-accelerate-environmental-land-management-schemes-with",
  "party": "Liberal Democrat",
  "policy_title": "Accelerate Environmental Land Management Schemes with Extra Funding",
  "outcome": "O12",
  "verdict": {
    "outcome": "O12",
    "direction": "worsens",
    "magnitude": "minor",
    "time_horizon": "this-parliament",
    "confidence": "moderate",
    "plain": "The policy commits an extra \u00a31 billion per year in public spending with no stated funding source, adding to borrowing. The spending supports productive environmental investment, but no evidence provided quantifies fiscal returns sufficient to offset the near-term cost.",
    "biggest_unknown": "Whether the extra \u00a31bn is funded by a tax rise or spending cut elsewhere, or is genuinely net new borrowing \u2014 the policy text states none.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "tier": "stated",
        "claim": "The policy commits an extra \u00a31 billion a year in new public spending on ELMS.",
        "evidence_id": "M",
        "quote": "properly funding it with an extra \u00a31 billion a year"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Defra's existing ELMS budget is projected to rise to \u00a32 billion by 2028/29 within an overall farming and nature recovery budget averaging \u00a32.7 billion per year from 2026/27 to 2028/29.",
        "evidence_id": "E7",
        "quote": "funding for ELMS is projected to rise to \u00a32 billion by 2028/29, within an overall farming and nature recovery budget averaging \u00a32.7 billion per year from 2026/27 to 2028/29"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "ELMS spending supports activities such as flood prevention and carbon sequestration that could reduce long-run public costs, though no fiscal return is quantified in the evidence provided.",
        "evidence_id": "E4",
        "quote": "mitigating and adapting to climate change (e.g., through carbon sequestration), preventing floods"
      }
    ],
    "rationale": "The policy adds \u00a31 billion per year to public spending. No funding mechanism \u2014 tax rise, bond issuance, or offsetting cut \u2014 is stated in the policy text or referenced in any of the provided evidence units. On O12, unfunded spending commitments worsen the near-term debt path symmetrically with unfunded tax cuts. The extra spending would raise ELMS funding by roughly 35\u201350% above the existing Defra trajectory (from ~\u00a32bn to ~\u00a33bn per year), a material but not transformative increment against total UK public spending. The investment case is genuinely plausible \u2014 ELMS funds flood resilience, carbon sequestration, and soil health, all of which could reduce future public costs \u2014 but none of the provided evidence units quantify a fiscal return that would offset the \u00a31bn annual cost on any realistic horizon. The NFU and environmental NGOs (advocacy sources, flagged accordingly) argue the current budget is already insufficient, which contextualises demand but does not address fiscal sustainability. The magnitude is scored minor because \u00a31bn represents roughly 0.04% of UK GDP and a small fraction of total public spending, limiting the debt-path effect even if entirely unfunded. The long-term picture is genuinely uncertain \u2014 productive environmental investment could in principle improve sustainability \u2014 but absent quantified evidence of fiscal returns, the near-term verdict on O12 is a modest worsening.",
